# Styling — Tailwind, `cn()`, cva & shadcn

**Impact: MEDIUM**

Prefer the existing design system and semantic primitives over one-off styling inventions.

## `className`

Static classes do not need `cn()`:

```tsx
<div className="flex items-center gap-2" />
```

Use `cn()` when classes are conditional, composed, or merged with caller-provided `className`:

```tsx
<div
  className={cn(
    "flex items-center gap-2",
    isActive && "bg-accent",
    className,
  )}
/>
```

A reusable component accepting `className` SHOULD merge its base classes with `cn()` so caller overrides resolve predictably.

## Variants

Use `class-variance-authority` when styling variants are a meaningful part of a reusable component's public API (`size`, `variant`, `intent`, etc.).

Do not create a `cva` definition for a single local boolean that is clearer as `cn(condition && "...")`.

## shadcn / Radix

Reach for existing shadcn/Radix primitives before hand-rolling dialogs, popovers, menus, tabs, form controls, tooltips and other interaction primitives.

`src/components/ui/` contains design-system/shadcn primitives only. Application/domain components do not live there.

Do not rewrite generated shadcn code merely to make it match application-component anatomy unless the repository intentionally owns/customizes that primitive.

## Tokens before arbitrary values

Prefer existing semantic tokens and Tailwind theme utilities (`bg-background`, `text-muted-foreground`, spacing/radius scale, etc.) over hard-coded hex values or arbitrary dimensions.

Use arbitrary values only when the design genuinely requires a value not represented by the system. Repeated arbitrary values SHOULD become a token/owned constant/theme decision.

## Responsive behavior

Use mobile-first responsive classes unless the repository/design system clearly follows another convention.

Do not duplicate entire components only to change a few responsive layout classes. Separate desktop/mobile components when behavior/structure genuinely differs, not just spacing.

## Shared styling

If multiple sibling components require the same non-trivial class recipe, place it at their nearest shared owner or model it as a reusable variant/component. Do not export unrelated shared styles from a component file just because that file used them first.
